And if you want to try it yourself here is a demo. Artists can experiment with new formulas to create their own. I even experimented with a couple of formulas. Research / experimentationIt could serve as a simple visualization environment for experimenting with families of interactive equations. UI developers can use the results for webpages to display them on.In general, everyone is interested in art. Or this type of art. Start by simple formulas for example: And expand with each step to become something like The purpose of the project is to show that mathematics could be used for very beautiful types of art. It's easy and fun and could show the beauty of mathematics. This project shows that art has many ways to be implemented. It's fun and could be done by anyone. Artist, programmers, and anyone interested. And now you can also create your own type of art. With this project.

In September 2024, War Robots support received a surge of complaints from Chinese iOS players: after updating to iOS 18, in-game text was displaying incorrectly for some users. The issue manifested differently across devices: some players noticed missing characters, while others reported wrong or unfamiliar glyph styles. To make matters worse, we were unable to reproduce the bug on our local test devices.My name is Sergey Madekin, Senior Developer on War Robots. In this article, I will share how we ultimately resolved the missing Chinese characters issue.Investigating the Root CauseUpon investigation, we discovered that our Chinese text rendering relied directly on system fonts. The game uses standard Unity UI (Text) components configured with dynamic fonts. Unity first searches for required glyphs within the designated font asset, then moves through the Font Names list, and finally falls back to the engine's built-in list of fallback system fonts for the given platform. It was this fallback mechanism that produced inconsistent results across different devices running iOS 18.A similar issue with missing characters in Legacy Text on iOS 18+ is logged in the Unity Issue Tracker. At the time of writing, no fix version is specified in the ticket.The First Attempt: Adding the Full FontThe fix itself is straightforward: bundle a dedicated Chinese font into the build and configure it as a fallback for missing glyphs. However, a complete Chinese font file contains tens of thousands of characters. Our base Noto Sans SC TTF file weighed around 10 MB, and adding it increased the final build size by 37 MB.We initially verified the fix by releasing a build with the full font, keeping in mind that we would optimize it later if it proved effective. The update resolved all text rendering issues with no further complaints, so I moved on to reducing the file size. Fortunately, daily communication requires only a fraction of the full character set, and a specific game requires even fewer.Optimization and Font Subsetting with pyftsubsetThe next challenge was keeping only the necessary characters in the source file. Luckily, the right tool already exists: pyftsubset, a command-line utility from Python's fontTools package designed to create slimmed-down font files containing only specified glyph sets. It discards unused glyphs and metadata, drastically shrinking the file size.All that remained was identifying which characters to keep. I started with a standard frequency list of common Chinese characters and ran the subsetting tool against it. However, gaps immediately appeared — particularly in localized in-game names, and there were quite a few of them. It became clear that we needed to extract the exact character set used in our text resources. I pulled all current translations and compiled a list of every unique character found in them. I then ran pyftsubset on the source file, keeping only this set. The results were impressive: out of roughly 30,000 characters in the original font, only about 3,000 remained, dropping the TTF size from 10 MB down to around 900 KB. The resulting build size increase was just 1.24 MB.Edge Cases and Player NicknamesWe shipped the next release with this lightweight font. Player complaints dropped drastically, but a few remained. We discovered that certain configuration files contained unlocalized strings with rare characters. Additionally, some players reported missing characters in their custom usernames.Because the missing character count was low, I created a dedicated supplementary list. While this resolved all known edge cases, it doesn't guarantee full coverage for future usernames containing obscure characters. Moving forward, whenever a new missing character is reported, we simply append it to the supplementary list and regenerate the font.Automating the Font Pipeline in UnitySince game content updates frequently, we needed an easy, automated way to update our character set before each release.After a few iterations, we built a custom Unity Editor tool that automates the whole process in a single click:Connects to the target test server hosting localization files for the upcoming release.Downloads the latest text resources.Extracts unique characters from the text and merges them with our supplementary list.Excludes glyphs already present in the primary game font to avoid duplication.Verifies character availability in the source Chinese TTF file. Missing glyphs are logged and skipped.(Optional) Includes the character set from the previous release if the "preserve old characters" setting is enabled.Runs pyftsubset on the source TTF file using the compiled character list and outputs the optimized font directly into the build.Preserving the previous release's character set prevents regression bugs if strings are removed from current localization files. While this causes the font size to grow slightly over time, it provides a solid balance between file footprint and backward compatibility.The Unicode Pitfall: One Character, Two CharsWhile refining the generator, we uncovered another nuance: collecting target characters using a  is unsafe.In C#, strings are UTF-16 encoded. Most Chinese characters we use fit into a single , but certain rare ones require two. Together, these two units form a surrogate pair representing a single character.A  stores individual unit values without preserving pairs. For instance, two distinct rare characters might share the same high surrogate. During deduplication, that shared unit is kept only once—reducing four  values down to a set of three. When passing that corrupted set to  as a string, neither of the original characters will be fully preserved.This behavior is demonstrated below:string text = "\U00020000\U00020001"; // Two distinct characters: 𠀀 and 𠀁

Console.WriteLine(text.Length);                   // 4 — char count
Console.WriteLine(new HashSet<char>(text).Count); // 3 — unique char units (corrupted)
Console.WriteLine(GetTextScalars(text).Count);   // 2 — actual Unicode characters
To preserve surrogate pairs intact, we introduced the  method. It iterates through the string and populates a  with complete characters: standard ones as a single- string, and surrogate pairs as a two- string.private static HashSet<string> GetTextScalars(string text)
{
    var result = new HashSet<string>();
    for (var i = 0; i < text.Length; i++)
    {
        if (char.IsHighSurrogate(text[i]) && i + 1 < text.Length && char.IsLowSurrogate(text[i + 1]))
        {
            result.Add(text.Substring(i, 2));
            i++;
        }
        else
        {
            result.Add(text[i].ToString());
        }
    }

    return result;
}
This safeguard protects our character set during subset preparation. Note that it is unrelated to the original iOS system font fallback issue.By building this workflow, we gained the ability to quickly update and expand our Chinese font while keeping the build size impact minimal. It now covers all game localizations, handles custom player names via our supplementary list, and optionally preserves coverage across past releases.

A room sketch can answer a small programming question: do two furniture footprints overlap, and does each footprint stay inside the room? Represent the room and each piece as rectangles, then run those checks whenever a coordinate changes. This gives you a repeatable way to compare a few layouts before ordering. Keep the sketch beside your measurements so that a change in the code can be checked against the room.Choose one coordinate systemUse centimetres throughout. Put the origin at the upper left corner of the plan, with x increasing to the right and y increasing downwards. Each rectangle has an x coordinate, a y coordinate, a width w and a height h. The coordinates describe its upper left corner. The width and height describe its footprint on the floor.The example uses a room measuring 420 by 320 centimetres. A sofa occupies a 200 by 90 rectangle at (40, 40). A table initially occupies a 100 by 60 rectangle at (160, 95). These dimensions are invented for the tutorial; they do not describe real products or a measured room. Axis-aligned rectangles keep the arithmetic short. A ninety-degree rotation swaps width and height; other angles require a different geometry test.Check overlap and room boundsTwo rectangles overlap when their horizontal intervals overlap and their vertical intervals overlap. The strict comparisons below count a shared edge as touching rather than overlap. Touching pieces have no gap between them, so a false overlap result alone says nothing about room to move, clean or open a drawer.Validate the numbers before comparing them. Number.isFinite rejects non-number values, NaN and infinities without converting strings. Positive widths and heights exclude empty or reversed rectangles. Negative coordinates remain valid inputs to the overlap function, while the room-bounds check rejects a piece placed beyond a wall.function checkBox(b) {
  if (!b || ![b.x, b.y, b.w, b.h].every(Number.isFinite)
      || b.w <= 0 || b.h <= 0) {
    throw new TypeError('Expected finite coordinates and positive dimensions');
  }
}

function overlaps(a, b) {
  checkBox(a); checkBox(b);
  return a.x < b.x + b.w && a.x + a.w > b.x
    && a.y < b.y + b.h && a.y + a.h > b.y;
}

function inside(box, room) {
  checkBox(box); checkBox(room);
  return box.x >= room.x && box.y >= room.y
    && box.x + box.w <= room.x + room.w
    && box.y + box.h <= room.y + room.h;
}

const room = { x: 0, y: 0, w: 420, h: 320 };
const sofa = { x: 40, y: 40, w: 200, h: 90 };
const table = { x: 160, y: 95, w: 100, h: 60 };
const movedTable = { ...table, y: 170 };
const route = { x: 310, y: 0, w: 90, h: 320 };
console.log(overlaps(sofa, table));       // true
console.log(overlaps(sofa, movedTable));  // false
console.log(inside(movedTable, room));    // true
console.log(overlaps(movedTable, route));// false
The first table position overlaps the sofa. Move the table to y = 170 and the overlap test returns false: the sofa ends at y = 130, leaving a forty-centimetre gap in this model. The table also passes the room-bounds check. Moving its x coordinate to 350 makes its right edge reach 450, beyond the room width of 420, so inside returns false.Reserve space that furniture should not occupyAdd a rectangle for a route through the room and test every furniture footprint against it. In the drawing, that route starts at x = 310 and has a width of ninety centimetres. This is an illustrative planning choice, not a prescribed clearance or an accessibility standard. The moved table ends at x = 260 and does not overlap the route. A shelf at (300, 20) with a width of forty would overlap it.Reserve another area near the door. The purple square in the drawing is a simplified keep-clear area measuring ninety by ninety centimetres at (0, 230). A real door rotates through an arc, and its handle, hinges and surrounding furniture need a separate check. Treat these reserved rectangles as visible assumptions. If an assumption changes, edit its dimensions and rerun the comparisons.Draw the same data in SVGSVG rectangles use x, y, width and height attributes, so the model maps directly to a drawing. Keep the room coordinates in a nested SVG viewBox and scale the display separately. In the figure, the red outline shows the initial table position; the green rectangle shows the moved position. Labels and colours help explain the two states without changing their dimensions.Illustrative dimensions in centimetres. Red outline: initial table. Green: moved table. Blue: reserved route. Purple: door keep-clear area.Replace the example data with checked measurementsMeasure the room, including radiators, projecting skirting and other fixed obstructions. Record the dimensions of the actual pieces you are considering. Record each item's product-page link beside its measurements so you can return to the same item while comparing layouts. Check each dimension before entering it into the model.A footprint check cannot tell you whether a piece will pass through an entrance, turn on a staircase or fit inside a lift. Compare the delivery route with the packaged dimensions separately. The model also omits height, sloping ceilings, doors and drawers in motion, and the shape of a person using a chair. Keep those checks alongside the rectangle results and revise the plan when the measured room or a chosen piece differs from the data. For a separate example of representing height in a projected drawing, see Creating Isometric Diagrams with SVG.Disclosure: The author works for Dantone Home.

A React support console displays . The Cypress test sees the banner and passes.The customer still has no refund.The failure is not necessarily in the assertion. The assertion observed exactly what it was written to observe: the browser rendered a successful state. The application, however, spans more than the browser. A write may fail after an optimistic update. A queue may accept a message that no worker processes. An audit event may never be emitted.For workflows with real consequences, I find it useful to treat the system as three independent witnesses. What did the user see?;  Banner, route, enabled state What did the system commit?;  REST or GraphQL response What happened afterward?;  Audit entry, queued notification, webhookAgreement among all three is stronger evidence than any one surface alone.Spend browser time only on the behavior under testSuppose the behavior is approving a refund. Logging in, creating an order, and moving it into an eligible state are prerequisites; they are not the behavior being evaluated. Repeating those operations through the UI makes the test longer and adds unrelated failure points.Cypress's  lets the same spec establish those prerequisites over HTTP. Its cookie jar is synchronized with the browser, so an API login can carry into the following .describe('refund approval', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.env(['supportUser', 'supportPassword']).then(
      ({ supportUser, supportPassword }) => {
        cy.session(
          ['support-user', supportUser],
          () => {
            cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', {
              username: supportUser,
              password: supportPassword,
            })
          },
          {
            cacheAcrossSpecs: true,
            validate: () => {
              cy.request('/api/auth/me').its('status').should('eq', 200)
            },
          },
        )
      },
    )

    cy.request('POST', '/test/fixtures/orders', {
      id: 'order-refund-17',
      total: 84.5,
      status: 'refund_eligible',
    })
  })

  it('commits the refund and records its audit event', () => {
    cy.visit('/support/orders/order-refund-17')
    cy.findByRole('button', { name: 'Approve refund' }).click()
    cy.findByRole('status').should('contain.text', 'Refund approved')

    cy.request('/api/orders/order-refund-17/refund')
      .its('body')
      .should('include', { status: 'approved', amount: 84.5 })

    waitForAuditEvent('order-refund-17').should('include', {
      action: 'refund.approved',
      actorType: 'support_user',
    })
  })
})
The test is still end-to-end. It simply uses the cheapest trustworthy surface for each responsibility:Browser for the user-critical decision.API for durable and downstream results.Poll asynchronous work, but put a boundary around itAudit entries and notifications may be created by a worker after the request returns. A single immediate assertion is therefore a race. An unbounded recursive retry is worse: it can conceal a stalled system until the CI job times out.Use an explicit attempt or time budget and preserve the last observed state in the error:function waitForAuditEvent(subjectId: string, attemptsLeft = 8) {
  if (attemptsLeft === 0) {
    throw new Error(`No refund.approved audit event for ${subjectId}`)
  }

  return cy
    .request(`/test/audit-events?subjectId=${subjectId}`)
    .then(({ body }) => {
      const match = body.events.find(
        (event: { action: string }) => event.action === 'refund.approved',
      )

      if (match) return match

      return cy
        .wait(500)
        .then(() => waitForAuditEvent(subjectId, attemptsLeft - 1))
    })
}
Eight attempts at 500 milliseconds is a four-second observation window. That number should come from the service-level expectation of the workflow, not from whatever value happens to make a flaky test pass.In a real system, I also include a correlation identifier in the fixture and query. Without one, a test may accidentally find an event produced by a different parallel test.GraphQL needs two success checksIf the durable-state API is GraphQL, HTTP status alone is weak evidence. GraphQL commonly returns  while including execution errors in the response body.cy.request('POST', '/graphql', {
  query: `
    query Refund($orderId: ID!) {
      refund(orderId: $orderId) { status amount currency }
    }
  `,
  variables: { orderId: 'order-refund-17' },
}).then(({ status, body }) => {
  expect(status).to.eq(200)
  expect(body).not.to.have.property('errors')
  expect(body.data.refund).to.deep.include({
    status: 'APPROVED',
    amount: 84.5,
    currency: 'USD',
  })
})
This verifies the transport, the GraphQL execution result, and the business state separately.Three Cypress mechanics that matter is convenient partly because it does  behave like application traffic in the browser. It does not appear in the browser's Network panelBrowser CORS rules do not apply: It cannot prove that the browser is allowed to call that origin cannot intercept it: Use  for requests made by the applicationCookies synchronize with the browser: API login can establish the browser sessionNon-2xx/3xx fails by default: Set  when testing an expected errorThis distinction prevents a common testing mistake. A direct  can prove that an endpoint works, but it cannot prove that frontend code called it with the correct headers. For that, observe the application request with  or validate the resulting server state after the UI action.Use Docker to reproduce the system boundary, not to fake productionThis pattern is easiest to trust when the browser, API, worker, and test runner start from a repeatable environment. A small Docker Compose stack can keep those process boundaries visible:services:
  app:
    build: .
    command: npm run start:test
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD", "node", "-e", "fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/health').then(r=>process.exit(r.ok?0:1)).catch(()=>process.exit(1))"]
      interval: 2s
      timeout: 2s
      retries: 30

  worker:
    build: .
    command: npm run worker:test

  cypress:
    image: "${CYPRESS_IMAGE:?use an approved full tag or digest}"
    working_dir: /e2e
    volumes:
      - ./:/e2e
    environment:
      CYPRESS_baseUrl: http://app:3000
    depends_on:
      app:
        condition: service_healthy
Pin  to a full Cypress Docker image tag or digest rather than  or a moving short tag. That keeps Node and browser changes from entering the comparison unnoticed. The worker remains a separate service, so a missing side effect cannot be hidden by running everything in one process.Compose is still not a production replica. It gives the test a controlled topology. It also makes one frequent mistake explicit: startup order is not readiness. The health check prevents Cypress from racing an application container that exists but cannot serve requests yet.Do not turn production internals into a public test APIThe example uses  and  endpoints. That does not mean such endpoints should be publicly reachable.Reasonable options include:exposing them only in an isolated test environment;requiring a narrowly scoped test credential;implementing database setup through  in the Node process;querying a test message sink rather than a production email provider;deleting fixtures by a per-test correlation ID.The point is observability, not bypassing authorization.What this pattern does not proveThis single workflow does not replace service contract tests, queue load tests, browser security tests, or production monitoring. It also does not prove that every downstream consumer handled the event—only the consumer or sink you chose to inspect.It does provide something narrower and valuable: a reviewable claim that one representative user action produced the expected presentation, durable state, and selected side effect.That is the reusable rule:Use the browser to prove the user journey, the API to prove committed state, and a bounded observation surface to prove asynchronous consequences.When those witnesses disagree, the test has found more than a red selector. It has found a system boundary worth debugging.

Most explanations of the OWASP API Security Top 10 stop at a definition and a one-line mitigation: "validate object ownership", "implement rate limiting". A developer who can recite those lines still ships the bug, because the line does not say where the check goes or what it looks like in code.This guide takes one small API and, for each risk in the 2023 edition (the current one), shows the code that breaks, the code that fixes it, and a test that keeps it fixed. The risks that cause the most damage get the most space. The last three are shorter, but each still ends in something you can paste.Everything below uses the same service: a multi-tenant store API on Node.js 20, Express and PostgreSQL (the  driver). An auth middleware sets  to .The customer who owns the orderA signed-in customer, own orders onlyThe signed-in user, a few fields onlyA signed-in customer, once per couponA merchant; the server fetches a URL they supplyEvery one of these routes has an obvious first implementation, and almost every obvious first implementation is on the OWASP list.API1: Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA)BOLA is an endpoint that checks who you are but not whether the object you asked for is yours. Here is the handler most people write first:app.get('/orders/:id', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await db.query('SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = $1', [req.params.id]);
  if (!rows[0]) return res.sendStatus(404);
  res.json(rows[0]);
});
 passes, so the route looks protected. Any signed-in customer can still read any order by changing the ID. Switching to UUIDs does not fix this: IDs leak through emails, logs, referrers and other endpoints.Fix 1: put ownership in the queryapp.get('/orders/:id', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, status, total_cents, created_at
       FROM orders
      WHERE id = $1 AND customer_id = $2`,
    [req.params.id, req.user.id]
  );
  if (!rows[0]) return res.sendStatus(404);
  res.json(toPublicOrder(rows[0]));
});
The ownership check is part of the lookup, so there is no window where the code holds a row it should not have. A missing order and someone else's order both return 404, which stops attackers from using 403 responses to map valid IDs.Fix 2: make the check impossible to forgetFix 1 works until someone adds a tenth endpoint in a hurry. Move data access behind a repository that cannot be called without a user:function ordersFor(user) {
  return {
    async findById(id) {
      const { rows } = await db.query(
        'SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = $1 AND customer_id = $2',
        [id, user.id]
      );
      return rows[0] ?? null;
    },
    async list({ limit, offset }) {
      const { rows } = await db.query(
        'SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT $2 OFFSET $3',
        [user.id, limit, offset]
      );
      return rows;
    },
  };
}

// in a handler
const order = await ordersFor(req.user).findById(req.params.id);
Handlers never import . A lint rule that bans  imports outside the repository folder turns a code-review habit into a build failure.Fix 3: a second layer in the databasePostgreSQL row-level security enforces the same rule even if application code gets it wrong:ALTER TABLE orders ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE orders FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

CREATE POLICY orders_owner ON orders
  USING (customer_id = current_setting('app.user_id')::uuid);
await client.query('BEGIN');
await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.user_id', $1, true)", [req.user.id]);
// queries here only see this user's rows
await client.query('COMMIT');
Two details decide whether this works. The application must connect as a role that is not a superuser, and  is needed because table owners bypass policies by default.test('a customer cannot read another customer\'s order', async () => {
  const order = await createOrder({ customer: alice });

  const res = await request(app)
    .get(`/orders/${order.id}`)
    .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(bob)}`);

  expect(res.status).toBe(404);
});
Write this test for every route that takes an object ID. The matrix near the end of this guide does it for all routes at once.API2: Broken AuthenticationAuthentication usually breaks in two places: token verification that accepts more than it should, and a login endpoint with no limit on guessing.The common mistakes are calling  (which checks nothing), verifying without pinning the algorithm, and skipping the issuer and audience checks. A token minted for another service then works on yours.const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const token = (req.headers.authorization || '').replace(/^Bearer /, '');
  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, PUBLIC_KEY, {
      algorithms: ['RS256'],
      issuer: 'https://auth.example.com',
      audience: 'orders-api',
      maxAge: '15m',
    });
    req.user = { id: payload.sub, role: payload.role, tenantId: payload.tid };
    next();
  } catch {
    res.sendStatus(401);
  }
}
Pinning  blocks the classic attack where a token is signed with HS256 using your public key as the secret.  caps token lifetime on the server side even if the issuer sets a long expiry. Keep access tokens short and use a refresh token that you can revoke.Limit login attempts per account and per IPA limit per IP alone fails against a botnet. A limit per account alone fails against password spraying, where one password is tried across thousands of accounts. Use both:const { RateLimiterRedis } = require('rate-limiter-flexible');

const byAccount = new RateLimiterRedis({
  storeClient: redis, keyPrefix: 'login_acct',
  points: 5, duration: 15 * 60, blockDuration: 15 * 60,
});
const byIp = new RateLimiterRedis({
  storeClient: redis, keyPrefix: 'login_ip',
  points: 50, duration: 15 * 60,
});

app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const email = String(req.body.email || '').toLowerCase();
  try {
    await Promise.all([byIp.consume(req.ip), byAccount.consume(email)]);
  } catch {
    return res.status(429).json({ error: 'Too many attempts. Try again later.' });
  }

  const user = await findUserByEmail(email);
  const hash = user ? user.password_hash : DUMMY_HASH;
  const ok = (await argon2.verify(hash, String(req.body.password || ''))) && !!user;
  if (!ok) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid email or password' });

  await byAccount.delete(email);
  res.json({ token: issueToken(user) });
});
Three choices in that handler matter. The error message is identical for an unknown email and a wrong password. A dummy hash is verified when the user does not exist, so response time does not reveal which emails are registered. The account counter resets on success so real users are not punished for one typo.The trade-off: a per-account limit lets an attacker lock a victim out for 15 minutes. If that matters for your product, replace the hard block with a CAPTCHA or an email challenge after five failures.test('rejects a token signed with the wrong algorithm', async () => {
  const forged = jwt.sign({ sub: alice.id, role: 'admin' }, PUBLIC_KEY, { algorithm: 'HS256' });
  const res = await request(app).get('/orders').set('Authorization', `Bearer ${forged}`);
  expect(res.status).toBe(401);
});

test('the sixth failed login is throttled', async () => {
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    await request(app).post('/login').send({ email: alice.email, password: 'wrong' });
  }
  const res = await request(app).post('/login').send({ email: alice.email, password: 'wrong' });
  expect(res.status).toBe(429);
});
API3: Broken Object Property Level AuthorizationThis risk has two halves: the API accepts fields the caller should not be able to write (mass assignment), and it returns fields the caller should not be able to read (excessive data exposure). Both come from passing whole objects through the handler.app.patch('/users/me', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const user = await User.update(req.user.id, req.body);
  res.json(user);
});
Send  and you are an admin. The response also returns , because the handler serializes the row as it came from the database.Fix the write side with an allowlistconst EDITABLE_BY_SELF = ['displayName', 'phone'];

function pickAllowed(body, allowed) {
  const unknown = Object.keys(body).filter((k) => !allowed.includes(k));
  if (unknown.length > 0) {
    const err = new Error(`Unknown fields: ${unknown.join(', ')}`);
    err.status = 400;
    throw err;
  }
  return Object.fromEntries(allowed.filter((k) => k in body).map((k) => [k, body[k]]));
}
Rejecting unknown fields with a 400 is better than dropping them silently. Silent dropping hides probing from your logs and hides client bugs from your own team. A schema library with a strict mode gives you the same behaviour plus type checks.Allowlists are per role, not per model. An admin editing a user may set ; a user editing themselves may not. Keep one list per (role, action) pair.Fix the read side with explicit serializersfunction toPublicUser(u) {
  return { id: u.id, displayName: u.display_name, phone: u.phone };
}

app.patch('/users/me', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const changes = pickAllowed(req.body, EDITABLE_BY_SELF);
  const user = await User.update(req.user.id, changes);
  res.json(toPublicUser(user));
});
A serializer lists what goes out. A blocklist (delete user.password_hash) lists what stays in, and it breaks the day someone adds a  column. Filtering in the client is not a fix at all: the full object is still in the response.test('a user cannot promote themselves', async () => {
  const res = await request(app)
    .patch('/users/me')
    .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`)
    .send({ role: 'admin' });

  expect(res.status).toBe(400);
  expect((await User.findById(alice.id)).role).toBe('customer');
});

test('responses contain only the public fields', async () => {
  const res = await request(app)
    .patch('/users/me')
    .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`)
    .send({ displayName: 'Alice' });

  expect(Object.keys(res.body).sort()).toEqual(['displayName', 'id', 'phone']);
});
The second test asserts the exact key set. It fails when anyone adds a field to the response, which forces a decision instead of an accident.API4: Unrestricted Resource Consumption"Add rate limiting" covers one of at least five limits an API needs. A request can be expensive because of how often it arrives, how large it is, how much it asks for, or how long it runs.A concrete limit configurationconst { rateLimit } = require('express-rate-limit');

app.set('trust proxy', 1); // exactly one proxy in front of the app

// 1. Body size: reject large payloads before parsing them
app.use(express.json({ limit: '100kb' }));

// 2. Unauthenticated traffic: per IP
app.use(rateLimit({ windowMs: 60_000, limit: 60, standardHeaders: true, legacyHeaders: false }));

// 3. Authenticated traffic: per user, so shared office IPs are not punished
const perUser = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 60_000,
  limit: 300,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
  keyGenerator: (req) => req.user.id,
});
app.use('/orders', requireAuth, perUser);
The  line is where this most often goes wrong. Without it, every request appears to come from your load balancer and all users share one bucket. Set too permissively, clients can spoof  and get a fresh bucket per request. Set it to the real number of proxies in front of the app.The default store is in memory, per process. With four instances, your real limit is four times the configured one. Use a shared store such as Redis in production.Cap what a single request can ask forapp.get('/orders', requireAuth, perUser, async (req, res) => {
  const limit = Math.min(Math.max(parseInt(req.query.limit, 10) || 20, 1), 100);
  const offset = Math.max(parseInt(req.query.offset, 10) || 0, 0);
  const orders = await ordersFor(req.user).list({ limit, offset });
  res.json(orders.map(toPublicOrder));
});
Without the clamp,  is a single request that passes every rate limit and still exhausts memory. The same applies to batch endpoints, GraphQL query depth, and file uploads: each needs its own maximum.Cap how long work can runconst pool = new Pool({
  max: 20,
  statement_timeout: 5_000,        // the database cancels slow queries
  connectionTimeoutMillis: 2_000,  // fail fast when the pool is exhausted
});

const server = app.listen(3000);
server.requestTimeout = 15_000;
server.headersTimeout = 10_000;
Timeouts turn a slow endpoint from an outage into an error. Without , twenty slow queries occupy the whole pool and every other endpoint stalls behind them.Operations that cost money per call (SMS, email, third-party lookups) need a spending limit as well as a request limit: a per-user daily cap and a billing alert at the provider.test('page size is capped at 100', async () => {
  await createOrders({ customer: alice, count: 150 });
  const res = await request(app)
    .get('/orders?limit=100000')
    .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`);
  expect(res.body.length).toBe(100);
});

test('oversized bodies are rejected', async () => {
  const res = await request(app)
    .patch('/users/me')
    .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`)
    .send({ displayName: 'x'.repeat(200_000) });
  expect(res.status).toBe(413);
});
API5: Broken Function Level AuthorizationBOLA is about which object; this one is about which action. A customer calls an admin endpoint and it works, usually because the only thing hiding that endpoint was the absence of a button in the UI.app.post('/admin/refunds', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  await issueRefund(req.body.orderId, req.body.amountCents);
  res.sendStatus(204);
});
The path says . Nothing in the code does.Fix: deny by default at the routerAdding  to this one handler fixes this one handler. The durable fix makes a missing check fail closed:function requireRole(...roles) {
  return (req, res, next) =>
    roles.includes(req.user?.role) ? next() : res.sendStatus(403);
}

const admin = express.Router();
admin.use(requireAuth, requireRole('admin'));   // applies to everything below
admin.post('/refunds', refundHandler);
admin.get('/users', listUsersHandler);
app.use('/admin', admin);
A new admin route added to this router is protected before its author thinks about it. For routes with mixed roles, declare the permission next to the route and refuse to start if one is missing:const routes = [
  { method: 'get',  path: '/orders/:id',    roles: ['customer'],          handler: getOrder },
  { method: 'post', path: '/admin/refunds', roles: ['admin'],             handler: refundHandler },
];

for (const r of routes) {
  if (!Array.isArray(r.roles) || r.roles.length === 0) {
    throw new Error(`Route ${r.method} ${r.path} declares no roles`);
  }
  app[r.method](r.path, requireAuth, requireRole(...r.roles), r.handler);
}
This table is also the input for the test matrix later in this guide.Two places this check gets skipped in practice: alternate HTTP methods on the same path ( is checked,  is not), and older API versions ( is protected,  still runs the old code).test('a customer cannot issue a refund', async () => {
  const res = await request(app)
    .post('/admin/refunds')
    .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`)
    .send({ orderId: order.id, amountCents: 100 });

  expect(res.status).toBe(403);
  expect(await refundsFor(order.id)).toHaveLength(0);
});
Assert the side effect as well as the status code. An endpoint that returns 403 after issuing the refund passes a status-only test.API6: Unrestricted Access to Sensitive Business FlowsHere the endpoint has no bug in the usual sense. It does what it was designed to do, and an attacker automates it: redeeming a coupon thousands of times, buying all the limited stock, creating accounts in bulk for referral credit. Authentication and authorization both pass.The fix belongs in the business rule, enforced by the database, with a rate limit as a second layer.CREATE TABLE coupon_redemptions (
  coupon_id   uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES coupons(id),
  customer_id uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES customers(id),
  redeemed_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  PRIMARY KEY (coupon_id, customer_id)
);
const redeemLimiter = new RateLimiterRedis({
  storeClient: redis, keyPrefix: 'redeem', points: 5, duration: 60 * 60,
});

app.post('/coupons/redeem', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  try {
    await redeemLimiter.consume(req.user.id);
  } catch {
    return res.sendStatus(429);
  }

  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query('BEGIN');

    const claimed = await client.query(
      `UPDATE coupons SET remaining = remaining - 1
        WHERE code = $1 AND remaining > 0 AND expires_at > now()
        RETURNING id`,
      [String(req.body.code)]
    );
    if (claimed.rowCount === 0) {
      await client.query('ROLLBACK');
      return res.status(409).json({ error: 'Coupon is not available' });
    }

    const inserted = await client.query(
      `INSERT INTO coupon_redemptions (coupon_id, customer_id)
       VALUES ($1, $2) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`,
      [claimed.rows[0].id, req.user.id]
    );
    if (inserted.rowCount === 0) {
      await client.query('ROLLBACK');
      return res.status(409).json({ error: 'Coupon already redeemed' });
    }

    await client.query('COMMIT');
    res.sendStatus(204);
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query('ROLLBACK');
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
});
The common broken version reads the coupon, checks  in JavaScript, then writes. Fifty parallel requests all read the same value and all pass the check. The single UPDATE ... WHERE remaining > 0 and the primary key move both rules into the database, where concurrent requests cannot both win.Rate limits per IP do little here because the traffic comes from many addresses with valid accounts. Limit per account, and add friction where abuse pays: verified email or phone before a coupon can be redeemed, and a delay or review step for high-value actions.test('fifty parallel redemptions succeed exactly once', async () => {
  const results = await Promise.all(
    Array.from({ length: 50 }, () =>
      request(app)
        .post('/coupons/redeem')
        .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`)
        .send({ code: 'WELCOME10' })
    )
  );
  expect(results.filter((r) => r.status === 204)).toHaveLength(1);
});
Raise the limiter's points for this test, or it will reject requests before the race is exercised.API7: Server Side Request Forgery (SSRF)Any endpoint that fetches a URL supplied by the caller lets the caller make requests from inside your network. Webhook testers, link previews, "import from URL" and PDF renderers are the usual places.app.post('/webhooks/test', requireAuth, async (req, res) => {
  const response = await fetch(req.body.url);
  res.json({ status: response.status, body: await response.text() });
});
Point this at http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ on a cloud VM and it returns instance metadata, which on a default configuration can include credentials. Point it at  and it reads your internal Elasticsearch.Why the obvious filters failA blocklist of strings such as  and  misses every other spelling of the same address. All of these reach loopback:Why a string filter misses itDecimal form of 127.0.0.1https://[::ffff:127.0.0.1]/https://internal.attacker.example/A public name whose DNS record points to 10.0.0.5https://safe.example/redirectA public page that redirects to an internal addressThe rule that survives these: resolve the hostname, check the resulting IP addresses, then connect to the address you checked.This version uses only Node's built-in modules.const dns = require('node:dns/promises');
const net = require('node:net');
const https = require('node:https');

const blocked = new net.BlockList();
for (const [addr, prefix] of [
  ['0.0.0.0', 8], ['10.0.0.0', 8], ['100.64.0.0', 10], ['127.0.0.0', 8],
  ['169.254.0.0', 16], ['172.16.0.0', 12], ['192.0.0.0', 24], ['192.168.0.0', 16],
  ['198.18.0.0', 15], ['224.0.0.0', 4], ['240.0.0.0', 4],
]) blocked.addSubnet(addr, prefix, 'ipv4');
for (const [addr, prefix] of [
  ['::', 127], ['64:ff9b::', 96], ['fc00::', 7], ['fe80::', 10], ['ff00::', 8],
]) blocked.addSubnet(addr, prefix, 'ipv6');

async function assertPublicUrl(raw) {
  const url = new URL(raw);
  if (url.protocol !== 'https:') throw new Error('https only');
  if (url.username || url.password) throw new Error('credentials not allowed');
  if (url.port && url.port !== '443') throw new Error('port not allowed');

  const host = url.hostname.replace(/^\[|\]$/g, '');
  const addresses = await dns.lookup(host, { all: true });
  if (addresses.length === 0) throw new Error('host did not resolve');

  for (const { address, family } of addresses) {
    if (blocked.check(address, family === 6 ? 'ipv6' : 'ipv4')) {
      throw new Error('address not allowed');
    }
  }
  return { url, address: addresses[0].address, family: addresses[0].family };
}
Every resolved address is checked, not just the first, because a name can return one public and one private record.  also matches IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses against the IPv4 rules, which closes the  route.Connect to the address you validatedValidating and then calling  resolves the name a second time. An attacker who controls the DNS server answers the first lookup with a public IP and the second with . This is DNS rebinding. Pin the connection to the checked address:function pinnedLookup(address, family) {
  return (hostname, options, callback) => {
    if (options && options.all) return callback(null, [{ address, family }]);
    callback(null, address, family);
  };
}

async function safeFetch(raw, { maxBytes = 64 * 1024, timeoutMs = 5000 } = {}) {
  const { url, address, family } = await assertPublicUrl(raw);

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const req = https.request(
      url,
      { method: 'GET', lookup: pinnedLookup(address, family), timeout: timeoutMs },
      (res) => {
        const chunks = [];
        let size = 0;
        res.on('data', (chunk) => {
          size += chunk.length;
          if (size > maxBytes) return req.destroy(new Error('response too large'));
          chunks.push(chunk);
        });
        res.on('end', () => resolve({ status: res.statusCode, body: Buffer.concat(chunks) }));
      }
    );
    req.on('timeout', () => req.destroy(new Error('timeout')));
    req.on('error', reject);
    req.end();
  });
}
 does not follow redirects, so a 302 to an internal address comes back as a 302 and nothing more. If you need redirects, follow them yourself and run  on every hop, with a cap of three or so. The TLS certificate is still verified against the original hostname, because only the DNS step is overridden.Do not hand the response backapp.post('/webhooks/test', requireAuth, requireRole('merchant'), async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { status } = await safeFetch(String(req.body.url));
    res.json({ delivered: status >= 200 && status < 300, status });
  } catch {
    res.status(400).json({ error: 'URL could not be reached' });
  }
});
Returning only the status code removes most of what an attacker gains even if a check is bypassed. The error message is generic on purpose: "connection refused" versus "timeout" is enough to port-scan an internal network.Application checks are one layer. Run URL-fetching workers in a subnet with no route to internal services, and enforce egress rules at the firewall. On AWS, require IMDSv2, which rejects the simple GET that most SSRF payloads send to the metadata endpoint.test.each([
  'http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/',
  'https://169.254.169.254/',
  'https://localhost/',
  'https://127.0.0.1/',
  'https://2130706433/',
  'https://0x7f.1/',
  'https://[::1]/',
  'https://[::ffff:127.0.0.1]/',
  'https://10.0.0.5/',
  'https://192.168.1.1/',
  'https://[fd00::1]/',
  'https://user:pw@example.com/',
  'https://example.com:8443/',
  'file:///etc/passwd',
])('rejects %s', async (url) => {
  await expect(assertPublicUrl(url)).rejects.toThrow();
});
Add one integration test with a hostname you control that resolves to a private address. It is the case a list of literal IPs cannot cover.API8, API9 and API10: the shorter threeThese three are less about one handler and more about how the service is configured, tracked and connected. Each still has a concrete control.API8: Security MisconfigurationThe three misconfigurations that show up most in Express services are open CORS, verbose errors, and debug routes left on.const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = new Set(['https://app.example.com']);

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const origin = req.headers.origin;
  if (origin && ALLOWED_ORIGINS.has(origin)) {
    res.set('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
    res.set('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
    res.set('Vary', 'Origin');
  }
  next();
});

// last middleware: one error shape for every failure
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  const status = err.status && err.status < 500 ? err.status : 500;
  if (status === 500) logger.error({ err, requestId: req.id });
  res.status(status).json({
    error: status === 500 ? 'Internal error' : err.message,
    requestId: req.id,
  });
});
Never reflect the request's  header back without checking it against a list. That is equivalent to a wildcard, and it works with credentials, which a literal  does not. The error handler returns a request ID instead of a stack trace: support can find the log line, and the caller learns nothing about your file paths or SQL.Misconfiguration is drift, so test it against the deployed environment, not just in unit tests:test('errors do not leak internals', async () => {
  const res = await request(app).get('/orders/not-a-uuid').set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokenFor(alice)}`);
  expect(JSON.stringify(res.body)).not.toMatch(/at \w+ \(|node_modules|SELECT/);
});

test('unknown origins get no CORS header', async () => {
  const res = await request(app).get('/orders').set('Origin', 'https://evil.example');
  expect(res.headers['access-control-allow-origin']).toBeUndefined();
});
API9: Improper Inventory ManagementYou cannot protect an endpoint you have forgotten. The typical finding is an old version (), a staging host with production data, or a debug route, none of which received the fixes applied to the main API.Make the OpenAPI document the source of truth and fail the build when the code disagrees with it:test('every registered route is documented', () => {
  const documented = new Set(
    Object.entries(openapi.paths).flatMap(([path, methods]) =>
      Object.keys(methods).map((m) => `${m} ${path.replace(/\{(\w+)\}/g, ':$1')}`)
    )
  );
  const undocumented = routes
    .map((r) => `${r.method} ${r.path}`)
    .filter((r) => !documented.has(r));

  expect(undocumented).toEqual([]);
});
This uses the  table from API5, which is a second reason to declare routes as data. For retired versions, set a removal date, send  and  response headers until then, and delete the code on that date. A version that is "deprecated" but still deployed is a live API.API10: Unsafe Consumption of APIsTeams validate user input and then trust a partner's API completely. A third-party response is input too. It can be compromised, wrong, slow or very large.async function getShippingRate(orderId) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://rates.partner.example/v1/quote/${encodeURIComponent(orderId)}`, {
    signal: AbortSignal.timeout(3000),
    redirect: 'error',
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`rates API returned ${res.status}`);

  const data = await res.json();
  const cents = data?.amountCents;
  if (!Number.isInteger(cents) || cents < 0 || cents > 1_000_000) {
    throw new Error('rates API returned an invalid amount');
  }
  if (!['INR', 'USD', 'EUR'].includes(data.currency)) {
    throw new Error('rates API returned an unknown currency');
  }
  return { amountCents: cents, currency: data.currency };
}
Four controls are in that function: a timeout, no redirects, validation of type and range, and a return value built from checked fields instead of the raw object. Strings from a partner API need the same treatment as user strings before they reach SQL, HTML or a shell.test('rejects a negative rate from the partner', async () => {
  mockRatesApi.reply(200, { amountCents: -500, currency: 'INR' });
  await expect(getShippingRate(order.id)).rejects.toThrow('invalid amount');
});
Testing strategy: an authorization matrix in CIIndividual tests cover the routes someone remembered to test. Authorization bugs live in the routes nobody remembered. A matrix test covers every route against every kind of caller, and fails when a new route is added without an entry.const actors = {
  anonymous: null,
  owner: () => tokenFor(alice),          // owns the fixture objects
  otherCustomer: () => tokenFor(bob),    // same role, different user
  otherTenant: () => tokenFor(carol),    // different tenant
  admin: () => tokenFor(adminUser),
};

const matrix = [
  // method, path,               anonymous, owner, otherCustomer, otherTenant, admin
  ['get',   '/orders/:orderId',  401,       200,   404,           404,         403],
  ['get',   '/orders',           401,       200,   200,           200,         200],
  ['patch', '/users/me',         401,       200,   200,           200,         200],
  ['post',  '/coupons/redeem',   401,       204,   204,           409,         403],
  ['post',  '/webhooks/test',    401,       403,   403,           403,         403],
  ['post',  '/admin/refunds',    401,       403,   403,           403,         204],
];

describe.each(matrix)('%s %s', (method, path, ...expected) => {
  test.each(Object.keys(actors).map((name, i) => [name, expected[i]]))(
    'as %s returns %i',
    async (actorName, status) => {
      const req = request(app)[method](path.replace(':orderId', fixtures.aliceOrder.id));
      const token = actors[actorName]?.();
      if (token) req.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
      const res = await req.send(fixtures.bodyFor(method, path));
      expect(res.status).toBe(status);
    }
  );
});

test('every route has a matrix row', () => {
  const covered = new Set(matrix.map(([m, p]) => `${m} ${p.replace(':orderId', ':id')}`));
  const missing = routes.map((r) => `${r.method} ${r.path}`).filter((r) => !covered.has(r));
  expect(missing).toEqual([]);
});
The expected values are specific to this example API; yours come from your own access rules. Three things make this pattern worth the setup:The "other customer" column is the BOLA test. Same role, valid token, wrong owner. It is the column most test suites lack.The last test is the important one. A new route with no row fails the build, so the person adding the route has to state who may call it.The matrix is readable by non-developers. A product owner can review a table of who can do what and spot a wrong cell.Around the matrix, a few cheaper checks catch other classes:Lint rule banning raw  imports in handlersResponse key-set assertionsRoute list compared with OpenAPIShadow and stale endpoints (API9)Dynamic scan with two user accounts against stagingWhatever the above missedHow these fail in productionThese bugs rarely look like bugs on the day they ship. They pass review and work for every legitimate user. The patterns below are the usual way they surface.The mobile app was the only client, until it was not. The app only ever requests the signed-in user's orders, so nobody notices the server does not check. Someone proxies the app's traffic, changes one ID, and gets another user's order. Nothing errors, so nothing alerts.The check exists on the new endpoint only. Version 2 was written carefully. Version 1 still runs for an old client, shares the same database, and has none of the fixes.The limit was per instance. Rate limiting worked in testing with one process. Production runs twelve behind a load balancer, each with its own in-memory counter.The internal tool was never meant to be public. An admin route had no role check because it sat on a private hostname. A routing change exposed it. A serializer returned the whole user row. Months later a migration added a  column, and the API began returning it.The feature was the vulnerability. "Import from URL" shipped as specified. The specification never said which URLs.The common thread is that every one of these returns HTTP 200. Error-rate dashboards stay flat. What does show them:One account receiving many 404s across sequential or varied object IDsEnumeration against object-level checks403s on admin paths from non-admin accountsProbing for function-level gapsMany accounts failing login with the same passwordOne account hitting a business endpoint far above the medianAutomated abuse of a sensitive flowOutbound requests from the API to private or metadata addressesLog the user ID, route, object ID and status for every authorization decision. Without those four fields, none of the signals above can be built.Every query for a user-owned object includes the owner in the  clauseHandlers cannot reach the database except through a user-scoped repositoryJWT verification pins the algorithm, issuer and audienceLogin is limited per account and per IP, with identical errors for unknown usersRequest bodies go through a per-role allowlist; unknown fields return 400Responses are built by serializers that list fields explicitlyBody size, page size, query time and request time each have a maximumRate-limit counters live in a shared storeAdmin routers apply the role check once, at the routerOne-per-user and limited-stock rules are enforced by database constraintsOutbound fetches resolve, check and pin the IP, and do not follow redirectsErrors return a request ID, never a stack traceCI fails when a route is missing from the OpenAPI document or the authorization matrixThird-party responses are validated and time-limited like user inputNone of these controls is advanced. What makes them work is where they sit: in the query, the router, the schema and the build, so that the secure behaviour is the default and the insecure one takes extra effort.
